[{:seon.task/name "an-outgoing-text-cites-what-it-names"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner wants one string parser run through what Seon says and reads, to pull in relevant context (2026-10-23). What exists: `seon.ref/parse` runs only on authored text (a task's plan, a wiki page, a docstring); an eval's printed answer, an error's text and a tool's output are never parsed, so an agent that sees `at seon.agent/error-map 3` or a stack frame must ask `(source seon.agent/error-map)` in a turn of its own. Measured 2026-10-23 on four error-shaped strings: parse already reads var names, keywords, `path#anchor` and `path:N` out of them (resolve-only, summary depth), and a four-space-indented stack frame (`    seon.agent/g (REPL:7)`) is read like any line, so no separate flat mode is needed for names (the log-line hypothesis did not reproduce; a position inside `(REPL:7)` is read as no citation, which `a-citation-can-carry-a-position` is about). Correct: at the one place an answer's text is made (`seon.ctx.wire/tool-text`, which `seon.repl/text-of` calls; a record keeps data and no printed text), run `seon.ref/parse` over that text and resolve its citations on the agent's db with `seon.ref/references`, the same resolution a task's plan gets, and show each hit at summary depth by `seon.ref/shown` under a budget (the key this plan first named is gone; a new key, if one is needed, goes in default.edn). It reuses the one parser and the one resolver: no second grammar, no per-kind code (the error's own :seon.error/in and :seon.error/at stay data, this reads unstructured text). A name that resolves to nothing is silent here (resolve-only), never an error. Replaces: the agent's follow-up `(source ..)` and `(doc ..)` turns for what the text already names; measured as turns to green on the e2e tasks before and after. Cost proof (work done, never time): per outgoing text, the contracted calls and bytes are linear in its characters and its candidate names, plus one index seek per resolved name; n = 100 names, and a text with no names costs one scan. Proofs: an item whose text names a var cites it; an unresolved name adds nothing; the cost proof. Wiki: references.md (a new section) and render.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-item-cites-the-vars-its-text-names seon.ref-test/parsing-an-outgoing-text-is-linear-in-its-names}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-citation-can-carry-a-position"
  :seon.task/plan "WAITS FOR OWNER RULING: new syntax (Root's gate #4); do not build before it. Idea: `at ns/f 3:6` reads today as the bare var `ns/f` and the position is dropped. Let a citation of a var carry the form-relative line and column that `:seon.error/at` already states (`(source v)` coordinates, 1-based) so context can show the var's recorded source with that line marked. Gate answers to bring the owner: (1) which existing mechanism does this: none, `path:N` is files only and marked stale, `seon.ref` has no position in a name; (2) v1: none; (3) the field: stack-frame `file:line` and compiler `file:line:col` are universal, but ours are form-relative on purpose; (4) why the error's own data cannot do it: it can for errors (`:seon.error/in`, `:seon.error/at`), this task is for text that is not an error map (a log, a plan, an agent's note). Plan if approved: one rule in `seon.ref/scan` beside `path:N`, a citation key for the position, a view that marks the line; positions stay out of stored facts (nothing stored depends on line numbers). Proof: ``seon.ref-test/a-var-with-a-position-shows-its-line``. Deletes nothing; do not add it if only error maps carry positions."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.ref/an-outgoing-text-cites-what-it-names"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ref-test/a-var-with-a-position-shows-its-line}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-resolver-names-a-thing"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by AGENTS section 3 step 4 its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: the agent tools take a name as a symbol, keyword, var, string or namespace and each decides which: seon.doc/named-under? strips a colon from text (7d299862fd, 92a4f76dd9, 0a58e8aa31); my.agent's ns-named? (4e8ffa1112) serves callers and callees but not lines or source, each with its own cond; prove's names are sniffed by qualification (twice a silent ran 0); seon.doc/ns-hit decides a test by the -test suffix though :var/meta holding :test is stored; example agents are found by the example/ prefix in three places. The change: one resolver in seon.ref, the one parser of citations, answering a name tagged by kind (var, ns, schema, section, task) or a no-var answer; every tool takes its answer, and :ns filters compare resolved namespace symbols, never text. Docs: docs/references.md and docs/agent.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ref-test/every-tool-names-a-thing-the-same-way}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-cite-is-a-ref-to-what-exists"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-09: a cite's target is a ref, ``:seon.cite/target``, to the cited entity, of six kinds: a doc section (:seon.doc/address), a var (:var/name), a task (:seon.task/id), a schema entry (:schema/key), a namespace (:ns/name) and a file (:file/path, or a namespace's :ns/file). No stub entities; the targets' required keys stay. The target is written as a lookup on its identity; a target that doesn't exist is refused with the typed error ``:seon.cite/no-target`` {:target [attr value]}, except when the same transaction creates it whole by tempid. A plan names what it will create as a two-backtick literal; library vars are literals for now. :seon.cite/address, name and task go; the cite's content id is from [citing entity, target identity]; the pattern's :by joins become one ref join. Dead citations become write refusals: the health class dead-citations and :seon.doc/dead-links go, and a wiki page whose link names no section is refused at its load. A cite whose target is only on its branch is held from syncing until the target goes up (stage 5's hold). Measured on main, 2026-11-09: of 1,919 cites, 414 name nothing that exists as their kind: 195 program files (valid with the file kind), 134 other addresses (build output, globs, placeholders, reference-code paths, a removed decisions section, test files by path), 65 Seon names that don't exist (mostly proofs a plan names before they're written), 53 library vars, 24 removed keywords. The sweep, in the same landing: future names become literals; removed names updated or deleted; build output, globs and placeholders become literals; library vars become literals; every change listed for review. State: being built on jolt/cite-ref (worktree .worktrees/cite-ref), lands in the erase window. Estimate about +35 -60 src plus the sweep's text edits; a store erase. Landing order (Optimization, agreed with Web, 2026-11-09; docs/plan.md's Now): (0) FIRST, jolt/index-arg with its Jolt :private? pin (``seon.source/analysis-never-writes-the-image``), as soon as the pin is proved: it is independent of the erase window, and 2a, 2b and stage 4 wait on it; (1) the erase window: the config in force as its own entity (``seon.config/the-config-in-force-is-its-own-entity``), Context's message-delivery re-model (no :seon.message/call), a cite is a ref (``seon.ref/a-cite-is-a-ref-to-what-exists``), with one store erase, Web and Context warned first; (2) jolt/sync (stage 5) with jolt/dbboot (1d) merged into it, landed as one, its up-merge reviewed before 1d goes in; (3) jolt/hiccup with stage 4, its consumer, after Web's browser check of the placeholders. Every landing message goes to the Web session (running point) before bin/land.

State, 2026-11-09 15:45 (Optimization): the sweep landed alone (58a5753b2b). Branch jolt/cite-ref, worktree .worktrees/cite-ref, head 78340aa41d plus a merge of main in progress, not landed: the ref, :seon.db/no-target, the dead-citation class removed, seeds for template targets in seon.db/seed-tx, a refused wiki page as a recorded problem with the boot going on (seon.root/wiki-boot!), a deleted cited var refused naming its cites, an ns/name cite a lookup on what the db holds after the write (the task the write makes, else the var). Context approved the model side. Next: the doc-cost fixture counts every section it stores (no bound move), prove, then into jolt/erase-window with Context's jolt/remodel-4, one store erase, Web and Context warned first. Owed to 2a: cite-hiccup and cited-name pick the kind by identity attribute."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
]
